seg-b14a11d60e9f = One to three dotted scenario keys, each over a linear or logarithmic range, on the same axis values the <c1/> kind uses. Every axis has a name and a unit; every metric has a name and a result path, and its unit is read from the swept kind's own units block unless the campaign declares one. With <c2/> every node is a seeded Monte Carlo ensemble (realisation <c3/> at the node's seed plus <c4/>), reported with the statistics below. <c5/> sweeps a jammer { "1" } km from the receiver from -50 to +{ "10" } dBW: the mean jammer-to-signal ratio moves exactly { "1" } dB per dB ({ "12.2" } to { "72.2" } dB), and availability falls from { "1" } to { "0" } between -30 and -25 dBW.

# text
# c1: system.2.sisre_m
# c2: sisre_m
# c3: [[system]]
# c4: case.0.system.0.shell.0.altitude_km
seg-c1931e311cca = A dotted key may index an array of tables by position: <c1/> is the <c2/> of the third <c3/> table, and <c4/> reaches three levels down.

# text
# c1: [monte_carlo]
seg-e774c578906d = <c1/>: a seeded ensemble

# text
# c1: k
# c2: base_seed + k
# c3: classical.series[-1].error_ns
seg-bbacb9c66f92 = Realisation <c1/> runs the scenario with its seed key set to <c2/>, the convention the clock ensemble already uses, so the ensemble reproduces exactly. Each metric reports the mean, the population standard deviation, the nearest-rank 5th, 50th and 95th percentiles, a fixed-seed percentile-bootstrap { "95" }% confidence interval on the mean ({ "2000" } resamples), and, by default, every sample in seed order. Result paths may index arrays: <c3/> is the last sample.

