seg-b57ec5e5168a = There are two methods:

# text
# c1: method = "grid"
# c2: steps
seg-8fba07ce0cde = <c1/> runs every node of a <c2/>-per-key grid, on the sweep's axis values.

# text
# c1: method = "nelder-mead"
# c2: max_evals
# c3: step
# c4: ftol
# c5: xtol
seg-844a7c1e3578 = <c1/> (the default) runs a deterministic Nelder–Mead simplex in coordinates normalised to [{ "0" }, { "1" }] per key. Trial points are projected onto the box. The section takes <c2/>, an initial <c3/>, and the stopping tolerances <c4/> and <c5/>.

# text
# c1: runs > 1
# c2: best_coords
# c3: best_metric
# c4: best_score
# c5: evals
# c6: stop_reason
# c7: trace
seg-2b1159b467f8 = A run whose metric is absent or non-finite scores worst, and it is kept in the trace. With <c1/>, the metric is the mean over the same seeds at every evaluation. The result gives <c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/>, <c5/>, <c6/>, and every evaluation in <c7/>.

